<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_ImpedimentNamesWhatItBlocks> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_ObligationsFollowStatus> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_PlanBeforeStart> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_ReadinessChecked> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_RepairReprioritises> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_ReviewNeedsControlRun> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_StateReflectsEvidence> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_StatusHistoryRecorded> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#OpeningProfile> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Adaptive lineage profile"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "NAAL"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Non-agentic adaptive lineage"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Adaptive> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The standard profile for a lineage that starts from an immature mission and matures it through time-boxed boxes: every obligation of the standard profile is kept, each register-level check is asked from the status that creates the element it checks, a coarse epic is decomposed by the time it is Ready, and the chain from mission to backlog is read from both ends with every re-pointing recorded. Built beside the standard and agentic profiles, which stay as they are; it becomes a default only on the Owner's confirmation after a live proof."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_AgentAccountability> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_CancellationAssessed> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_ChangeSupportsMission> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_ConcurrentLineages> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_DecompositionStateAgrees> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_FlowPointsRecorded> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_ImpedimentNamesWhatItBlocks> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_PlanBeforeStart> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_RepairReprioritises> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_StatusHistoryRecorded> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#OpeningProfile> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Agentic lineage profile"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Agentic> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The standard profile plus accountability for agent work: an agent named for any function of an item is a run of a definition, every status move names who made it, and the parties that do, oversee, verify and approve an item are different."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_CancellationAssessed> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_ChangeSupportsMission> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_ConcurrentLineages> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_DecompositionStateAgrees> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_FlowPointsRecorded> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_ImpedimentNamesWhatItBlocks> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_PlanBeforeStart> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_RepairReprioritises> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://example.org/backlog#profileIncludes> <http://example.org/backlog#RS_StatusHistoryRecorded> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#OpeningProfile> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Standard lineage profile"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#Profile_Standard> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The way of working every lineage can adopt at its opening: status moves and flow points are recorded, a repair re-prioritises the items it touches, a standing impediment names the work it blocks, and a parent whose children are finished is not left open."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_AgentAccountability> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_1> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_AgentAccountability> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_AgentAccountability> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_AgentAccountability> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Agent work is accounted for"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_AgentAccountability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Within the adopter's scope: an agent named for any function of a work item is a run (ExecutorInstance) of a definition, not the definition; every status move names who made it (transitionedByExecutor); the parties that do, oversee, verify and approve one item are four different parties, and agent runs of one definition count as one party; a Done item is overseen, verified and approved; an item at or above the adopter's humanApprovalLevel is approved by a person or a human team; an agent team or federation named for an item was assembled by a recorded act with its seats held by runs; a delegated lineage is spawned by the executor responsible for the item that delegated it. A RuleSet because it binds only an adopter (G89, G91). This package's own register does not adopt it."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_CancellationAssessed> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_3> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_CancellationAssessed> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_CancellationAssessed> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_CancellationAssessed> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A lineage is set down only after an as-is assessment"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_CancellationAssessed> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An Abandoned lineage (cancelled or failed) has an as-is assessment that sorts every work item as accomplished, failed, in progress or due and every objective as fitted or gapped, states the fit-gap and the impact, and a closure report marked cancelled or failed that cites it. The Owner's rule of 2026-10-26 (G162); Abandoned is the framework's one exit for it, labelled Cancelled as well."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_CeremonyKinds> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_5> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_CeremonyKinds> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_CeremonyKinds> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_CeremonyKinds> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A ceremony record names the one kind of work it serves"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_CeremonyKinds> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Within the adopter: every refinement event, planning ceremony, review ceremony and retrospective names exactly one kind of work, from the closed set of eight."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ChainBothWays> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_4> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ChainBothWays> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ChainBothWays> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ChainBothWays> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "The chain from mission to backlog reads the same from both ends"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ChainBothWays> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Within the adopter: an item that is started or Done may satisfy only a deliverable that a scope statement of the lineage requires, so a chain element discovered from the bottom (a story found while working) is joined to the top before it counts; and re-pointing a chain element (a mission, goal, objective, scope statement or work item) is recorded as a ChainRevision with its reason, the objective it serves, the role that decided it and the box boundary it follows. Elements may be created in any order; the chain is read from both ends at every box boundary. The Owner's rule of 2026-10-27 (G166): the connection is conserved, the creation order is not one-directional."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ChangeSupportsMission> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_3> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ChangeSupportsMission> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ChangeSupportsMission> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ChangeSupportsMission> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Every change request supports an objective of the mission"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ChangeSupportsMission> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A change request names, by supportsObjective, the objective of the running mission it serves. Change management adapts the running lineage for missing, incorrect or unmatching items; a request that supports no objective is another mission. The Owner's rule of 2026-10-26 (G162)."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ConcurrentLineages> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_2> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ConcurrentLineages> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ConcurrentLineages> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ConcurrentLineages> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "At most one active lineage unless they are connected"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ConcurrentLineages> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Two lineages may be active at once (status Scoped to InProgress, or Revived) only when each is maintained by a different party (maintainedBy: an individual, project, program or portfolio, G159) or when connected: a divide-and-conquer part and its parent (parentLineage), or a work item of one that dependsOn a work item of the other, followed transitively. Otherwise one runs and the other waits as a lineage at LS_Opened, the native queue, and advances only after the running lineage is Achieved. The owner's rules of 2026-10-12 (G104) and 2026-10-26 (G159). Every lineage opened with a profile adopts it (both profiles include it), so a new lineage is single by default; registers, fixtures and closed graphs that never adopted it are untouched (G89, G91)."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_DecompositionStateAgrees> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_1> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_DecompositionStateAgrees> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_DecompositionStateAgrees> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_DecompositionStateAgrees> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A parent whose children are all finished is not left open"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_DecompositionStateAgrees> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Within the adopter's scope: a work item whose children are all Done or Cancelled, with at least one Done, is itself Done or Cancelled. The state the children derive and the state asserted must agree; the rule reports the disagreement as a violation. A RuleSet because it binds only a register or lineage that declared it (G89, G91)."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_DigestTable_v5> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_8> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_DigestTable_v5> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_DigestTable_v5> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_DigestTable_v5> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Stage digest table v5 (obligation artifacts are not stage content)"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_DigestTable_v5> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The v4 digest table without Specification, TestScenario, ModelArtifact and AcceptanceCriterion. Under v5 an item that a ScopeChange admits is also not part of the Backlog digest (G157): the owner-decided change record is the visibility, and a change-managed addition after the close would otherwise leave the digest failing for good. A register adopts it and re-records its stage digests as a stated migration, never silently (G91); a re-recorded digest witnesses the state at the re-recording, not the state at the original close, and the migration is disclosed as such."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_FlowPointsRecorded> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_1> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_FlowPointsRecorded> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Kanban flow measures need a well-defined start and finish point (The Kanban Pocket Guide, ch. 6); ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2026 measurement process: a measure is to be verifiable, meaningful, actionable, timely and cost-effective. The detail standard ISO/IEC/IEEE 15939 was not read."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_FlowPointsRecorded> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_FlowPointsRecorded> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_FlowPointsRecorded> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Done work records when it started and finished, and how exactly"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_FlowPointsRecorded> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Every Done item in scope carries startedAt and finishedAt, or says through flowPointAbsenceReason why it does not; a flow point written as midnight states flowPointPrecision Date; a Proposed item carries no startedAt and only a Done or Cancelled one carries a finishedAt (G115); startedAt and finishedAt each have one value (a correction replaces, never adds); and a start equal to the finish, or closer than the adopter's shortSpanThreshold, carries a shortSpanExplanation (an equal pair is the signature of an item registered after the work). Adopted by a register or by one lineage (G112); a lineage adoption binds only that lineage's work and the items it admitted, so it can start at a stated moment without judging earlier work. A RuleSet because it binds only what declared it (G89, G91). This package's own lineages adopt it themselves; the shipped data file does not adopt it for a register (G116)."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ImpedimentNamesWhatItBlocks> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_1> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ImpedimentNamesWhatItBlocks> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ImpedimentNamesWhatItBlocks> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ImpedimentNamesWhatItBlocks> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A standing impediment names the work it blocks"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ImpedimentNamesWhatItBlocks> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Within the adopter's scope: an unresolved impediment impedes at least one work item or container. A wait that names no work is a question, and an unrelated question does not hold a lineage (the Owner's rule of 2026-10-24). A RuleSet because it binds only a register or lineage that declared it (G89, G91)."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_IndependentVerification> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_IndependentVerification> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_IndependentVerification> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Done work is verified by a party other than its executor"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_IndependentVerification> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Every work item that reads Done names, through verifiedBy, an Executor that is independent of the one that produced it (not the responsible Executor, not a member of it, not a collective that contains it). A register or one lineage may adopt it; a lineage adoption binds only that lineage's work and the items its output admitted, and a register adoption may state ruleSetAdoptedAt, before which finished items are exempt (G112). The adopter may state a verificationThreshold, and then only Done items whose integrityLevel reaches it need a verifier (an unclassified Done item is refused, UnclassifiedDoneShape). A RuleSet because it binds only what declared it, so closed work outside the scope is not judged retroactively (G89, G91). The independence of a verifier that is named is checked everywhere, adopted or not (VerifierIndependenceShape). This package's own register does not adopt it."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ObligationsFollowStatus> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_4> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ObligationsFollowStatus> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ObligationsFollowStatus> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ObligationsFollowStatus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A register-level obligation is owed from the status that creates it"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ObligationsFollowStatus> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Within the adopter: the checks that a register holds a goal for the mission, an objective, a scope statement, a blueprint, a definition of done, a commitment of the register and a member item for every container are owed from the lineage status at which that element is made (a goal from Goaled, an objective from Objectived, a scope statement and blueprint from Scoped, the rest from Backlogged), not from the first commit. A lineage that starts from an immature mission matures stage by stage; no obligation is removed, each is asked at the status that creates it and for ever after. The Owner's rule of 2026-10-27 (G166): simplify how a lineage is operated, never the obligations it owes. A coarse epic with no children is likewise owed its decomposition only once it is Ready."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ObligationsFollowStatus> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote> "Addendum, release 1.440.0 (found by Lineage 20, the first lineage opened under the adaptive profile): the life-cycle sweep of each domain entity (every life-cycle stage covered by a backlog item or a recorded gap) is also owed from Backlogged, the status at which the items that cover an entity are first made; it was asked at Scoped, where the entity is owed and no such item can yet exist. The rule set is otherwise as defined."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_PlanBeforeStart> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_3> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_PlanBeforeStart> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_PlanBeforeStart> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_PlanBeforeStart> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Work starts only after the plan step"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_PlanBeforeStart> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A lineage's work may start only after a kick-off for the lineage that activates a roadmap report computed from the register no later than the kick-off. The plan step sits between the Backlog stage and the first start; urgent work and items a restart or the change-management route admits are not asked for it. The Owner's rule of 2026-10-26 (G162). Every lineage opened with a profile adopts it; lineages that never adopted it are untouched (G89, G91)."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ReadinessChecked> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_6> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ReadinessChecked> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ReadinessChecked> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ReadinessChecked> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A ready item has a readiness check with every criterion met"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ReadinessChecked> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Within the adopter: an item that is Ready has a recorded readiness check, and no criterion of that check is unmet."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_RepairReprioritises> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_1> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_RepairReprioritises> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_RepairReprioritises> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_RepairReprioritises> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A repair re-prioritises the open items it touches"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_RepairReprioritises> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Within the adopter's scope: an open defect either names, through reprioritises, the open work items whose priority it changes, or states through reprioritisesNothingBecause why none; and every open item it names carries a priority score computed after the defect's own. A RuleSet because it binds only a register or lineage that declared it (G89, G91); the owner's rule of 2026-10-23 is that a repair entering the backlog re-ranks the open items rather than merely being added."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ReviewNeedsControlRun> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_7> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ReviewNeedsControlRun> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ReviewNeedsControlRun> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ReviewNeedsControlRun> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A review is refused while a planned control of its box has no run"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_ReviewNeedsControlRun> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Within the adopter: a sprint review of a box is refused while a control activity planned into that box has no recorded run."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_StateReflectsEvidence> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_9> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_StateReflectsEvidence> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_StateReflectsEvidence> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_StateReflectsEvidence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A work item's state reflects the evidence it carries"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_StateReflectsEvidence> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "A work item that still reads Proposed, Ready or Refined may not carry evidence the bridge has verified (a test that passed, a hash that matched, an artifact that exists): the work is there and the register says it has not started. A RuleSet because it binds only registers that declared it, so a register whose items were never moved is not judged retroactively (G89, G91). This package's own register does not adopt it."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_StatusHistoryRecorded> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_1> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_StatusHistoryRecorded> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Kanban flow measures need a well-defined start and finish point (The Kanban Pocket Guide, ch. 6); the framework's own TransitionEvent, whose definition says a state with no history cannot be audited."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_StatusHistoryRecorded> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_StatusHistoryRecorded> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_StatusHistoryRecorded> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Every status change is recorded, with its moment"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_StatusHistoryRecorded> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "An item that has left Proposed has a recorded move into its current state, and the earliest recorded move leaves Proposed (G115: a history that begins at Ready or In Progress has skipped the states before it); one that has started has a recorded move into In Progress; the recorded moves of an item chain without a gap (each move leaves the state the previous one entered); every move is stamped with an instant, not a date written as midnight; and startedAt and finishedAt, when the item is In Progress or Done, equal the moment of the first move into In Progress and of the last move into Done. Suspension and resumption are moves like any other. Adopted by a register or by one lineage (G112 scoping); an item that moved before the adoption says so through statusHistoryAbsenceReason, and nothing excuses a move after it. A RuleSet because it binds only what declared it (G89, G91)."@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_VerifierSession> <http://example.org/backlog#introducedInRevision> <http://example.org/backlog#Pr_11> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_VerifierSession> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/backlog#RuleSet> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_VerifierSession> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual> .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_VerifierSession> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "A verifier run records its session, as the Agentic-Systems package defines it"@en .
<http://example.org/backlog#RS_VerifierSession> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Within an adopting lineage, every run named as verifiedBy for a work item records: the way it was started, the grant of access it started under (who gave it, to which place, when), the verification plan it followed with that plan's digest, the session that carried it out, whether the place it read was unchanged, and a recorded configuration of the verifier definition made before the run; the grant is given before the run starts; and a person is the grantor when the verified item is at or above the lineage's humanApprovalLevel. The terms are those of the Agentic-Systems package, version 0.78.0, used by link and not minted again. A RuleSet because it binds only lineages that adopt it when they open (G184)."@en .
